CertHub.register({"id":"ckad","vendor":"The Linux Foundation / CNCF","name":"Certified Kubernetes Application Developer","short":"CKAD","exam":"CKAD (Kubernetes v1.35 curriculum)","blurb":"Hands-on Kubernetes exam for developers who design, build, configure, deploy, expose and troubleshoot cloud native applications on a cluster.","status":"verified","statusNote":"Domains and weights read on Sept 24, 2026 in the CNCF CKAD Curriculum v1.35 PDF (github.com/cncf/curriculum): Application Design and Build 20%, Application Deployment 20%, Application Observability and Maintenance 15%, Application Environment, Configuration and Security 25%, Services and Networking 20%. The Linux Foundation training site could not be opened from here, so exam length (2 hours, 15–20 tasks) and the 66% pass mark come from the Linux Foundation FAQ as quoted in search results; confirm them on the exam page before booking.","lastVerified":"2026-09-24","notices":[],"examInfo":{"questions":"About 15–20 performance-based tasks in a live Kubernetes command line","minutes":120,"pass":"66%","extra":"The real exam has no multiple-choice questions: you solve tasks with kubectl, YAML, Helm and Kustomize on real clusters, with the Kubernetes docs open. The practice exam on this site is a 60-question, 90-minute knowledge check of the same objectives; pair it with timed hands-on practice in kind or minikube (and the killer.sh simulator sessions included with registration)."},"examSim":{"questions":60,"minutes":90},"sources":[{"label":"CNCF CKAD Curriculum v1.35 (PDF, GitHub)","url":"https://github.com/cncf/curriculum/blob/master/CKAD_Curriculum_v1.35.pdf"},{"label":"Linux Foundation CKAD exam page","url":"https://training.linuxfoundation.org/certification/certified-kubernetes-application-developer-ckad/"},{"label":"Linux Foundation CKA/CKAD/CKS FAQ","url":"https://docs.linuxfoundation.org/tc-docs/certification/faq-cka-ckad-cks"}],"planWeeks":8,"hoursPerWeek":"6–8","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Application Design and Build","w":20,"topics":["Writing Dockerfiles: base images, layers, multi-stage builds, ENTRYPOINT vs CMD","Building, tagging, saving and loading images with docker or podman (build, tag, save, load)","How Pod `command` and `args` override the image ENTRYPOINT and CMD","Choosing a workload: Deployment, StatefulSet, DaemonSet, Job, CronJob, bare Pod","Jobs: completions, parallelism, backoffLimit, activeDeadlineSeconds, restartPolicy Never/OnFailure","CronJobs: schedule syntax, concurrencyPolicy, history limits, running a job manually from a CronJob","Multi-container patterns: init containers, sidecars (including native sidecars with restartPolicy: Always), adapter and ambassador","Ephemeral volumes: emptyDir (including medium: Memory), configMap/secret/projected volumes","Persistent storage: PersistentVolume, PersistentVolumeClaim, StorageClass, access modes, dynamic provisioning","Mounting volumes with volumeMounts, mountPath, subPath and readOnly"],"notes":["Curriculum: define, build and modify container images; choose workload resources; multi-container Pod patterns; persistent and ephemeral volumes"],"labs":["Write a two-stage Dockerfile for a small Go or Python app, build it with `docker build -t demo:1.0 .`, load it into kind with `kind load docker-image demo:1.0` and run it as a Pod.","In kind or minikube, create a Job with `completions: 6` and `parallelism: 2`, watch it with `kubectl get pods -w`, then turn it into a CronJob and trigger it with `kubectl create job manual --from=cronjob/<name>`.","Build a Pod with an init container that writes a file into an emptyDir and a sidecar that tails a log file the main container writes; then add a PVC (minikube's default StorageClass) and prove the data survives Pod deletion."]},{"id":2,"name":"Application Deployment","w":20,"topics":["Deployments and ReplicaSets: how the Pod template, labels and selectors fit together","Rolling updates: maxSurge, maxUnavailable, minReadySeconds; the Recreate strategy","kubectl rollout status, history, undo (--to-revision), pause and resume; kubectl set image and scale","Blue/green deployments by switching a Service selector between two Deployments","Canary releases with two Deployments behind one Service, weighted by replica count","Helm basics: repositories, charts, releases; helm repo add/update, search, install, upgrade, rollback, uninstall, list","Helm values: helm show values, --set and -f values.yaml, helm template, namespaces with -n and --create-namespace","Kustomize: kustomization.yaml, resources, namePrefix, namespace, commonLabels/labels, images, patches, configMapGenerator","Applying overlays with kubectl apply -k and previewing with kubectl kustomize"],"notes":["Curriculum: deployment strategies (blue/green, canary); Deployments and rolling updates; Helm; Kustomize"],"labs":["Create a Deployment with `kubectl create deployment web --image=nginx:1.26 --replicas=4`, update it with `kubectl set image`, watch `kubectl rollout status`, then roll back with `kubectl rollout undo` and read `kubectl rollout history`.","Build a blue/green setup: deployments web-blue and web-green with label `version`, one Service; switch traffic with `kubectl patch svc` and check with `curl` from a busybox Pod. Then turn it into a 3:1 canary.","Install a public Helm chart into a new namespace, change a value with `helm upgrade --set`, roll back with `helm rollback`; then make a Kustomize base plus a `prod` overlay that changes replicas and the image tag."]},{"id":3,"name":"Application Observability and Maintenance","w":15,"topics":["API deprecations and removals: finding current apiVersions with kubectl api-resources, api-versions and explain","Updating manifests to supported API groups (networking.k8s.io/v1 Ingress, batch/v1 CronJob, autoscaling/v2 HPA)","Liveness, readiness and startup probes; httpGet, tcpSocket, exec and grpc handlers; timing fields","What each probe failure does: restart the container vs remove the Pod from Service endpoints","kubectl get, describe, get events, top pod/node (metrics-server) for monitoring","Container logs: kubectl logs with -c, -f, --previous, --tail, --since, -l and deploy/<name>","Reading Pod status: Pending, ImagePullBackOff, CrashLoopBackOff, OOMKilled, Completed, exit codes","Debugging: kubectl exec, kubectl debug (ephemeral containers and Pod copies), port-forward, temporary busybox Pods","Output tricks for fast troubleshooting: -o wide, -o yaml, jsonpath, --show-labels, --sort-by"],"notes":["Curriculum: API deprecations; probes and health checks; built-in CLI monitoring tools; container logs; debugging"],"labs":["Add liveness, readiness and startup probes to an nginx Deployment in kind; break the readiness path and watch the Pod leave `kubectl get endpointslices`, then break liveness and watch the restart count rise.","Install metrics-server in minikube (`minikube addons enable metrics-server`), run a CPU-burning Pod and compare `kubectl top pod` with its requests and limits; then cause an OOMKilled with a low memory limit and read it in `kubectl describe`.","Deploy three deliberately broken Pods (bad image tag, crashing command, missing ConfigMap) and fix each using only `kubectl describe`, `kubectl logs --previous`, `kubectl get events` and `kubectl debug`."]},{"id":4,"name":"Application Environment, Configuration and Security","w":25,"topics":["Extending Kubernetes: CustomResourceDefinitions, custom resources and Operators; discovering them with kubectl get crd and api-resources","Request flow: authentication, authorization (RBAC) and admission control (mutating and validating)","RBAC objects: Role, ClusterRole, RoleBinding, ClusterRoleBinding; kubectl auth can-i with --as","Resource requests and limits for CPU and memory; units (m, Mi, Gi); QoS classes","Namespace ResourceQuota and LimitRange defaults","ConfigMaps: create from literals, files and env files; consume as env, envFrom and volumes","Secrets: generic, docker-registry and tls types; base64 encoding vs encryption; secretKeyRef and volume mounts","ServiceAccounts: serviceAccountName, token projection, automountServiceAccountToken, kubectl create token","SecurityContext at Pod and container level: runAsUser, runAsNonRoot, fsGroup, readOnlyRootFilesystem, allowPrivilegeEscalation","Linux capabilities (add/drop) and Pod Security Admission levels (privileged, baseline, restricted)"],"notes":["Curriculum: CRDs and Operators; authentication, authorization and admission; requests, limits and quotas; ConfigMaps; Secrets; ServiceAccounts; SecurityContexts and capabilities"],"labs":["In kind, create a namespace with a ResourceQuota and a LimitRange, try to create Pods with and without requests, and explain each rejection or default you see.","Create a ConfigMap and a Secret from literals and a file, consume them as env vars and as a volume in one Pod, change the ConfigMap and see which view updates.","Create a ServiceAccount, a Role that can only list Pods and a RoleBinding; check with `kubectl auth can-i --as=system:serviceaccount:<ns>:<sa>`, then run a Pod as non-root with a read-only root filesystem and all capabilities dropped."]},{"id":5,"name":"Services and Networking","w":20,"topics":["Service types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, ExternalName and headless (clusterIP: None)","Selectors, port vs targetPort vs nodePort, named ports","EndpointSlices and why an empty endpoint list means the selector or readiness is wrong","Cluster DNS names: <service>.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local","kubectl expose, kubectl create service and kubectl port-forward","NetworkPolicy basics: podSelector, policyTypes, ingress and egress rules, default deny","NetworkPolicy peers: podSelector, namespaceSelector, ipBlock; AND vs OR rule semantics; allowing DNS on egress","NetworkPolicy needs a CNI plugin that enforces it (for example Calico or Cilium)","Ingress resources: ingressClassName, host and path rules, pathType Prefix vs Exact, default backend, TLS secrets","Ingress controllers (for example ingress-nginx) and testing with curl and Host headers"],"notes":["Curriculum: basic NetworkPolicies; provide and troubleshoot access via Services; Ingress rules"],"labs":["Expose a Deployment with `kubectl expose` as ClusterIP and NodePort, reach it from a busybox Pod by DNS name, then break the selector and diagnose it from the empty EndpointSlice.","Start minikube with `--cni=calico`, apply a default-deny ingress policy to a namespace, then allow only Pods labeled `role=frontend` to reach the backend on port 8080 and test with `wget` from two Pods.","Enable the minikube ingress addon, create an Ingress that routes /api and /web to two Services on one host, and test with `curl -H 'Host: demo.local' http://$(minikube ip)/api`."]}],"study":{"1":[["When would you choose a Job, a CronJob, a Deployment, a StatefulSet or a DaemonSet?","Job: run-to-completion work. CronJob: a Job on a schedule. Deployment: stateless replicas with rolling updates. StatefulSet: stable Pod names and one PVC per replica (databases, quorum systems). DaemonSet: one Pod per node (log or monitoring agents)."],["How do Pod `command` and `args` relate to a Dockerfile's ENTRYPOINT and CMD?","`command` replaces ENTRYPOINT and `args` replaces CMD. If you set only `args`, the image's ENTRYPOINT runs with your arguments; if you set `command` alone, the image's CMD is ignored."],["What is the difference between an init container and a sidecar?","Init containers run one after another to completion before app containers start (setup, waiting for dependencies). A sidecar runs alongside the app for the Pod's life (log shipping, proxies); native sidecars are init containers with restartPolicy: Always so they start first and keep running."],["Compare emptyDir, hostPath and a PersistentVolumeClaim.","emptyDir lives and dies with the Pod and is shared by its containers. hostPath mounts a node directory, tying the Pod to that node and posing a security risk. A PVC requests durable storage that outlives the Pod and is bound to a PV, often provisioned dynamically by a StorageClass."],["Why use a multi-stage Dockerfile?","Build tools and sources stay in an earlier stage; only the compiled artifact is copied into a small runtime image. The result is smaller, faster to pull and has less attack surface."]],"2":[["Explain maxSurge and maxUnavailable with 4 replicas, maxSurge 1 and maxUnavailable 0.","Kubernetes may run up to 5 Pods (4 + 1 surge) and never fewer than 4 available. It starts one new Pod, waits for it to be ready, removes an old one and repeats, so capacity never drops."],["How do you do blue/green and canary with plain Kubernetes objects?","Blue/green: two full Deployments with different version labels; the Service selector points at one and you switch it in one change. Canary: both Deployments share a label the Service selects, and the replica ratio (for example 9 to 1) sets roughly how much traffic the new version gets."],["What does `kubectl rollout undo` actually do?","It rolls the Deployment's Pod template back to an earlier ReplicaSet revision (the previous one, or --to-revision=N), which starts a normal rolling update back to that template."],["Helm release vs chart vs repository?","A chart is a package of templates and default values; a repository hosts charts; a release is one installed instance of a chart in a cluster with its own name, values and revision history."],["What problem does Kustomize solve and how is it applied?","It customizes plain YAML without templates: a base plus overlays that add prefixes, labels, namespaces, image tags and patches. Render with `kubectl kustomize <dir>` and apply with `kubectl apply -k <dir>`."]],"3":[["Contrast liveness, readiness and startup probes.","Liveness failure makes the kubelet restart the container. Readiness failure removes the Pod from Service endpoints without restarting it. A startup probe holds off the other two until the app has started, which protects slow-starting apps from early liveness restarts."],["A Pod is in CrashLoopBackOff. What do you run first?","`kubectl describe pod` for events, exit code and last state, then `kubectl logs <pod> --previous` (add -c for multi-container Pods) to read the output of the crashed instance."],["How do you find the right apiVersion for a resource on the current cluster?","`kubectl api-resources` lists kinds with their group/version; `kubectl explain <kind>` shows the preferred version and fields; `kubectl api-versions` lists all served group/versions."],["What does `kubectl debug` add over `kubectl exec`?","It can inject an ephemeral container with debugging tools into a running Pod (useful for distroless images with no shell), make a debug copy of a Pod with changes, or start a debugging Pod on a node."],["What does exit code 137 with reason OOMKilled tell you?","The container used more memory than its limit and the kernel killed it (137 = 128 + SIGKILL 9). Raise the memory limit or fix the app's memory use."]],"4":[["Describe the path a kubectl request takes through the API server.","Authentication (who are you: certificates, tokens), then authorization (may you do this: RBAC), then admission control (mutating then validating admission, such as quotas, Pod Security Admission, webhooks), then persistence to etcd."],["How do requests and limits behave for CPU and memory?","The scheduler places Pods using requests. At runtime CPU above the limit is throttled, while memory above the limit gets the container OOMKilled. Requests equal to limits for every container gives the Guaranteed QoS class."],["ResourceQuota vs LimitRange?","ResourceQuota caps totals for a namespace (for example total requests.cpu or number of Pods). LimitRange sets per-container defaults and min/max. With a CPU quota in place, Pods without requests are rejected unless a LimitRange supplies defaults."],["Are Secrets encrypted?","Not by default: values are only base64-encoded in the API. Protect them with RBAC, encryption at rest configured on the API server, and by mounting only what each Pod needs."],["Why set automountServiceAccountToken: false and a dedicated ServiceAccount?","Most apps never call the API, so not mounting a token removes a credential an attacker could steal. A dedicated ServiceAccount with a narrow Role gives apps that do need the API only the permissions they need."],["Which securityContext settings form a typical hardened container?","runAsNonRoot: true (with a non-zero runAsUser), allowPrivilegeEscalation: false, readOnlyRootFilesystem: true, capabilities drop [\"ALL\"], and seccompProfile type RuntimeDefault, which together meet the restricted Pod Security Standard."]],"5":[["Explain port, targetPort and nodePort on a Service.","port is what clients use on the Service's ClusterIP; targetPort is the container port traffic is sent to; nodePort (30000–32767 by default) opens the same port on every node for NodePort and LoadBalancer Services."],["A Service exists but curl to it times out. How do you troubleshoot?","Check `kubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=<svc>`: empty means the selector does not match Pod labels or Pods are not ready. Then verify targetPort matches the container port and that no NetworkPolicy blocks the traffic."],["What does a default-deny ingress NetworkPolicy look like?","A policy with `podSelector: {}` (all Pods in the namespace), `policyTypes: [Ingress]` and no ingress rules. Allowed traffic is then added with further policies, which are additive."],["In a NetworkPolicy `from` list, what is the difference between one element with podSelector and namespaceSelector and two separate elements?","In one element they are ANDed: Pods with that label in namespaces with that label. As two list items they are ORed: any Pod with the label in the policy's namespace, or any Pod in matching namespaces."],["What do you need for an Ingress to work?","An Ingress controller running in the cluster (such as ingress-nginx), an Ingress whose ingressClassName matches it, rules pointing at existing Services and ports, and a TLS Secret if you terminate HTTPS."]]},"qCount":90,"hasLessons":true,"hasLessonsEs":true,"hasPbqs":true});
